irectories, \'curl\' for downloading the binary, and \'vault\' for configuration. The variables like vault_version and vault_policies are passed correctly.\n\nI should also check that the playbook starts with \'---\' and that each task is properly indented and structured in YAML. Since the user wants it included in an Ansible Role, the tasks/main.yml file should be correctly placed, but in the output, I just need to provide the YAML content.\n\nOne thing to be careful about is the use of \'Vault\' in the CN field of the O=HashiCorp/CN=Vault. I\'m not entirely sure how this relates to the playbook, but it seems to be part of the user\'s instructions, so I\'ll include it as part of the setup.\n\nI should also ensure that all variables and paths are correctly specified. For example, the extracted Vault binary should be placed in /usr/local/bin, and the configuration files in /etc/vault.d. The hash ring file is created with \'nodes\' and \'weights\', and the policy is defined with \'path", "explicit denying" etc.\n\nFinally, I\'ll make sure that the service is started and enabled on boot using the \'systemd\' module, which is appropriate for most Linux systems.\n\nPutting it all together, I\'ll structure the playbook with the correct YAML syntax, ensuring each task follows the specified order and uses the exact module calls and variables provided by the user. I\'ll avoid adding any comments or explanations, keeping the output clean and strictly adhering to their instructions.\n</think>\n\n```yaml\n---\n- name: Install Vault dependencies\n  apt:\n    name:\n      - unzip\n      - curl\n    state: present\n\n- name: Download and install Vault\n  file:\n    path: /usr/local/bin/vault\n    src: "https://releases.hashicorp.com/vault/{{ vault_version }}/vault_{{ vault_version }}_linux_amd64.zip"\n    state: present\n    mode: \'0755\'\n\n- name: Create Vault configuration directory\n  file:\n    path: /etc/vault.d\n    state: directory\n\n- name: Generate Vault hash ring\n  h