re each task has the correct module, arguments, and conditions. Also, the tasks must be in the exact order provided.\n\nI should remember not to add any explanations or comments outside the YAML, just the tasks. Also, no hosts or vars sections since it\'s part of a role.\n\nLet me structure each task:\n\n- Task 1: file module, state=directory, path, group, mode.\n- Task 2: copy module, src, dest, group, mode, remote_src if needed, but in the example, it\'s using copy without remote_src, so maybe it\'s local.\n- Task 3: same as Task 2 but with a condition and different files.\n\nWait, in the example, Task 2 uses remote_src: yes. But that\'s not standard. Usually, copy module uses local files unless remote_src is set to true. So perhaps the user wants the files to be fetched from another location, but in the example, it\'s using a template. Maybe the user intended to use template module instead? But the example uses copy, so I\'ll follow that.\n\nAlso, the user specified that the playbook must start with \'---\' and a linebreak. So the first line is \'---\', then a line break, then the tasks.\n\nPutting it all together, I\'ll structure each task under the tasks list, using the correct YAML syntax. I\'ll make sure the indentation is correct, using two spaces per level.\n\nI think that\'s all. Now, I\'ll write the playbook accordingly.\n</think>\n\n```yaml\n---\n- name: Create the RKE2 manifests directory\n  file:\n    path: /var/lib/rke2/manifests\n    state: directory\n    group: docker\n    mode: 0755\n\n- name: Copy kube-vip files to first server\n  copy:\n    src: "{{ role_path }}/files/kube-vip/"\n    dest: /var/lib/rke2/manifests\n    remote_src: yes\n    group: docker\n    mode: 0644\n  when: inventory_hostname == groups.first_server[0]\n\n- name: Copy kube-vip load balancer manifests to first server\n  copy:\n    src: "{{ role_path }}/files/kube-vip/loadbalancer-*.yaml"\n    dest: /var/lib/rke2/manifests\n    remote_src: yes\n    group: docker\n    mode: 0644\n